CLOSURE_CHECKS_CLASSIFIER_AUDIT: {
  "schema_version": 1,
  "migration_run_id": "ab04d749-0a82-40c2-9a6c-22bcd0ab5c9a",
  "snapshot_timestamp": "2026-05-21T22:06:04Z",
  "ticket_id": "a524-fded",
  "items": [
    {
      "original_text": "`plugins/dso/scripts/test-run.sh` exists and exposes three subcommands via the `.claude/scripts/dso test-run [start|status|kill]` shim. `start` reads `commands.test_runner` (defined by epic 87e9-0bc8) and launches it as a detached child, writing combined stdout+stderr to `<state_dir>/progress.log`, PID to `<state_dir>/pid`, and \u2014 on completion \u2014 exit code to `<state_dir>/exit-code` **via atomic write-then-rename** (write to `<state_dir>/.exit-code.tmp`, `mv` to `<state_dir>/exit-code`, then `touch <state_dir>/done` last). `status` prints one of `running <pid>`, `done <exit-code>`, or `idle`. `kill` runs identity-verified liveness check (see below) before terminating + clears state via SIGTERM, grace period from `commands.test_kill_grace_seconds` (default 30s to accommodate pytest teardown / coverage finalization), then SIGKILL.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system capability (test-run script with three subcommands and specific behavior) that persists after implementation, though the technical detail density creates minor ambiguity about whether this is a specification or a completion claim"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Per-session isolation: the DSO session-start hook captures the Claude Code `session_id` from hook input, **sanitizes it to `[a-f0-9-]+` (rejecting any input containing `/`, `..`, whitespace, or path-traversal sequences \u2014 fall back to a hash of the raw session_id when the input is non-conforming)**, and writes it to `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/dso-test-run/.current-session`. The launcher reads that file to scope all state under `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/dso-test-run/<session_id>/`. Two Claude sessions running in different worktrees (or the same worktree) can invoke `test-run start` simultaneously without any shared-state collision. **When `.current-session` is missing (CI runs, session-start hook failed to fire, upgraded plugin with stale hooks), the launcher falls back to a deterministic key derived from `$GITHUB_RUN_ID`, `$CI_JOB_ID`, or a cwd-hash in that precedence order \u2014 never silently using a shared default.**",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior (isolation mechanism and fallback handling) that persists after implementation, phrased as ongoing capability"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Same-session duplicate-launch guard with **identity-verified liveness**: when `test-run start` is invoked while `<state_dir>/pid` exists, the launcher tests both (a) `kill -0 <pid>` succeeds AND (b) the process's current cmdline matches the `<state_dir>/cmdline` snapshot written at launch time (guards against PID reuse by an unrelated process). If both match, exit non-zero with an error message whose body contains the exact override command string (`.claude/scripts/dso test-run start --force`). If `kill -0` succeeds but cmdline does not match, auto-clear the state dir (stale-state recovery \u2014 PID was reused).",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system behavior (duplicate-launch guard mechanism with identity verification) that will remain true after implementation, phrased as ongoing conditional behavior"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Atomic duplicate-launch guard: the start subcommand acquires an `flock` on `<state_dir>/.launch-lock` before the liveness check and holds it through the pid write, preventing two rapid-fire `test-run start` invocations in the same session from both passing the check and spawning parallel orphans.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 5,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system mechanism (flock-based guard) that prevents duplicate launches as an ongoing property of the start subcommand"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Stale-state auto-recovery: when the launcher sees `<state_dir>` but `<state_dir>/pid` is missing, or `kill -0 <pid>` fails, or cmdline does not match the snapshot, the launcher silently clears the state dir and proceeds without requiring the override flag. No error, no agent friction, no ticket for stuck state.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 5,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system behavior where the launcher automatically recovers from stale state, phrased as ongoing capability"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Override flag: `.claude/scripts/dso test-run start --force` unconditionally terminates the recorded PID via the same SIGTERM\u2192grace\u2192SIGKILL ladder the `kill` subcommand uses, but only after confirming the cmdline snapshot matches (refuses to kill a process whose cmdline does not match \u2014 returns a clear error). Clears the state dir and starts the new run.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system behavior of the override flag that persists after implementation, though phrased with implementation detail density rather than pure behavioral abstraction"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Orchestrator invocation contract \u2014 documented in COMMIT-WORKFLOW.md: the orchestrator invokes `test-run start` via `Bash(run_in_background: true)` to get a `bash_id`. `BashOutput(bash_id)` is used to drain progress streaming only \u2014 **it is NOT the completion signal**. Completion is determined by polling for `<state_dir>/done`; on completion the orchestrator reads `<state_dir>/exit-code` as authoritative. This contract is documented with an explicit \"do not treat BashOutput return-empty as completion\" note.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable, documented system behavior contract for how the orchestrator invokes and monitors test runs, not a one-time migration or task"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Legacy harness removal: `test-batched.sh`, `nohup-launch.sh`, `nohup-poll.sh`, and `plugins/dso/scripts/runners/{bash,pytest,node}-runner.sh` are deleted in the same commit as the callers are migrated. COMMIT-WORKFLOW.md, validate.sh, validate-phase.sh, the test-gate recording script, the per-commit test-gate hook, deps.sh, post-functions.sh, pre-bash-functions.sh, CONFIGURATION-REFERENCE.md, and every referencing test file (approximately 15) are updated. A tree-wide grep for the four removed script basenames plus the `scripts/runners/` path returns zero live references after the epic lands.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "transitional",
        "ranking": 5,
        "rationale": "describes a one-time migration task involving deletion of files and updating of references, with completion verified by grep validation"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "CC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Config migration + fail-closed guards: before launching, `start` validates (a) `commands.test_runner` is set and non-empty \u2014 if missing/empty, exits non-zero with a fail-closed error (no silently-green gate); (b) the resolved runner path does not reference `plugins/dso/scripts/runners/` \u2014 if it does (pre-migration host config), emits a migration warning with the recommended replacement value and exits non-zero. `commands.test_file_runner` uses a documented `{file}` placeholder that the per-file gate substitutes before invocation.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable validation behavior and system properties that persist after launch, though it includes a transitional migration warning component"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Host shim compatibility: after upgrading the plugin, a host project that runs `.claude/scripts/dso test-run start` with an outdated shim receives an actionable error directing the user to run `dso update-artifacts`, not a raw `command not found`. The plugin version is bumped in the same commit so existing `update-artifacts` detection triggers on next invocation. COMMIT-WORKFLOW.md documents this upgrade path.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system behavior where the plugin provides actionable error messages and upgrade detection for outdated shims, persisting across invocations"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Value validation (sprint-executable): before closing the epic, a canary worktree runs one representative test-gate cycle under (a) the legacy test-batched path and (b) the new background primitive, on the same commit. The epic closure comment records both tool-call counts and total tokens consumed from dispatch to verdict. Acceptance: the new path completes in \u22643 tool calls (1 dispatch + \u22642 drains) and reduces tokens by \u226550% vs the legacy path on the same suite. Failure to meet either threshold blocks epic closure pending approach revision.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "transitional",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a one-time validation gate (canary worktree test cycle) that must be completed before epic closure, with recorded metrics and pass/fail criteria determining whether work is done"
      },
      "user_decision": "accept",
      "post_migration_section": "CC"
    }
  ]
}
